---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { TYPES } from '../../data/types';
import { itemList, faqPage } from '../../lib/schema';

const PATH = '/careers/';
const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Can a personality test tell me what job to do?',
    answer: 'Not on its own, and be wary of any test that claims otherwise. Personality type has a weak relationship with job performance. What it usefully describes is the working conditions you are likely to find sustaining or draining, which narrows a search rather than deciding it.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What predicts job satisfaction better than personality type?',
    answer: 'Vocational interest. The six-theme Holland model — the basis of most professional careers guidance — predicts satisfaction and how long people stay in a role considerably better than type does. Our free career fit test measures it in 36 questions.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I rule out a career because my type is not listed for it?',
    answer: 'No. Every type is represented in every profession. These pages describe where a type tends to find the work sustaining, not where they are permitted to go. Interest, skill, and circumstance all matter more.',
  },
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Careers by Personality Type — All 16 Guides"
  description="Career guides for all 16 personality types: how each type works, where careers stall, whether management fits, and how to run a job search. Free, no signup."
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'Careers', path: PATH }]}
  schema={[
    itemList('Career guides by personality type', TYPES.map((t) => ({ name: `${t.code} careers`, path: `/careers/${t.code.toLowerCase()}/` }))),
    faqPage(faqs, PATH),
  ]}
>
  <section class="hero wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">16 career guides</p>
    <h1>Careers by personality type</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      One guide per type, covering how that type actually behaves in a job, the career trap it
      most often falls into, whether management is worth pursuing, and how to run a search.
      Written to be useful rather than flattering.
    </p>
    <div class="btn-row">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/tests/career-fit/">Take the career fit test</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/tests/personality-type/">Find your type first</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>Pick your type</h2>
      <p>Each guide is a standalone read — you do not need to have taken the test.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="grid grid-4">
      {TYPES.map((type) => (
        <a class="card" href={`/careers/${type.code.toLowerCase()}/`}>
          <h3><span class="type-code">{type.code}</span> careers</h3>
          <p>{type.careers.slice(0, 3).map((c) => c.title).join(' · ')}</p>
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section tint">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Read these guides with the right expectation</h2>
      <p>
        There is a large gap between what personality-type career content usually promises and
        what the underlying research supports. The honest version is this: type is a
        <strong>weak predictor of job performance</strong> and a
        <strong>moderate predictor of job satisfaction</strong>. It tells you relatively little
        about whether you could do a job well, and rather more about whether the daily experience
        of it would wear you down.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is still worth something. Most people who leave a role do so because of conditions —
        interruption, isolation, politics, pace, whether the work has a visible point — rather than
        because they could not do the tasks. Those conditions are exactly what type describes.
      </p>
      <p>
        If your actual question is "what should I do for work", the better instrument is the
        <a href="/tests/career-fit/">career fit test</a>, which measures vocational interest. Use
        the type guides to understand <em>how</em> you want to work, and the interest profile to
        narrow <em>what</em> you work on.
      </p>

      <h2>Common questions</h2>
      {faqs.map((faq) => (
        <details><summary>{faq.question}</summary><p>{faq.answer}</p></details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
